    headlineTail: "— owner-driver tours & taxi, Kangra",
    updated: "2026-11-16",
    stackLine: "Astro 7 · TypeScript · OSRM distances · Self-hosted AVIF · Vercel",
    service: { href: "/services/travel-website-development/", label: "Travel website development" },
    title: "Kangra Valley Travels — Tour & Taxi Website · Divyansh Sood",
    description: "A 158-page tour and taxi site for an owner-driver in Kangra: map-measured distances, a build that blocks unconfirmed claims, and booking on WhatsApp.",
    ogTitle: "Kangra Valley Travels — owner-driver tours & taxi · Case study",
    ogDescription: "158 pages for a Kangra owner-driver, and a build that won't publish a claim he hasn't confirmed.",
    ogImage: "https://www.divyanshsood.com/images/og/kangra-valley-travels.jpg",
    keywords: "Kangra taxi website, Gaggal airport taxi website, Kangra tour packages website, owner driver taxi website, taxi website developer Himachal, local SEO taxi, OSRM distances, Astro travel website, Divyansh Sood",
    feature: { src: "/images/work/kangra-valley-travels-1.webp", alt: "Kangra Valley Travels — homepage hero, desktop" },
    body: [
      { tag: "The brief", title: "One man, one car, two jobs", type: "prose",
        html: `At launch Sahil owned and drove one car: a white Maruti Suzuki Ertiga, registration HP 01 DA 1601, six passengers plus the driver and a roof carrier. He picks up at Gaggal (Kangra) Airport and the Amb Andaura and Pathankot stations, and drives one-to-five-day tours of the valley. He is also one of the two owner-drivers behind <a href="/projects/baglamukhi-travels/">Baglamukhi Travels</a>, which already has its own site. That made two jobs: compete with aggregators and directories for “Kangra tour and travel”, and do it without copying the sister business or competing with it for the same searches.` },
      { tag: "Stack", type: "list", items: ["Astro 7, static output", "TypeScript", "Self-hosted AVIF hero, WebP photo ladder", "Plus Jakarta Sans, one 27 KB file", "TravelAgency, TouristTrip, TaxiService, Person and Car schema"] },
      { tag: "The person", title: "Sell the driver, not a fleet", type: "prose",
        html: `Aggregators sell “a sedan”. At launch Sahil sold himself and one car, so the site said so everywhere. “About Sahil” sits in the main menu, there's a page for the car, and its own words are: <em>“It is the car in every photograph on this site.”</em> The plate number appears in the captions. The photos are Sahil's own and the Ertiga's, used with the owners' agreement, and none of the sister business's other drivers, cars or review photos. Structured data describes a <code>Person</code> and a <code>Car</code>, not an anonymous fleet.` },
      { tag: "Since launch", title: "More cars, the same driver", type: "prose",
        html: `Sahil now books more than his own Ertiga: the site offers an <strong>Ertiga, Dzire, Etios, Crysta and Tempo Travellers</strong>, up to 17 passengers, and says the cabs are AC — claims the build blocked until he confirmed them. Every booking still goes to him on WhatsApp, with one fare agreed first, so the site keeps selling the person rather than a call centre. The site has grown with the business: in November 2026 its sitemap lists <strong>158 pages</strong>, including <strong>49 taxi route pages</strong>, 9 town taxi pages (Palampur, Bir, Dharamshala and others), 35 tour and yatra packages and 22 destinations.` },
      { tag: "Fact checks", title: "A build that won't guess", type: "prose",
        html: `Before the site is built, a script scans the source and fails on any rupee figure, “per km”, “24×7”, “AC” or “air-conditioned”, “no advance”, “free cancellation”, “N years of experience”, a star rating, review or offer markup, a phone number outside the one config file, or a distance typed by hand without a cited source. Every one of those is something taxi sites routinely say, and every one was something Sahil hadn't confirmed yet. Dated offers are built in, rendered through one formatter, and switched off: the list is empty, so the offers page doesn't exist until he names a deal.` },
      { tag: "Distances", title: "Distances from the map, not from memory", type: "prose",
        html: `At launch, thirty-two named places were geocoded from OpenStreetMap and run through OSRM, giving <strong>558 road distances</strong>, each stored with the date checked and the source. OSRM gave noticeably different answers in each direction for 34 of 496 pairs, mostly around Masroor and Pong Dam, so every leg used the shorter of the two. The data now holds 70 places, plus about a hundred legs read off Google Maps directions; where Google has a figure, every page prints that one, so a road shows the same distance everywhere. No distance is typed into a page, and a leg nobody measured fails the build. OSRM's drive times are never published: on hill roads, a drive time is a promise Sahil would have to keep. Where a leg was read off Google Maps, the page quotes Google's “typically 35–50 min” range and names Google as its source.` },
      { tag: "The sister site", title: "Two businesses, one driver, no copying", type: "prose",
        html: `A duplication check runs on the built site. No block of text over 120 characters may appear on two pages, and any two pages sharing more than 45% of their eight-word runs fail as doorway pages. It also compares every page against the sister site's build and fails on any 12-word run that appears in both. The keyword map stays out of the searches the sister site already serves.` },
      { tag: "Keywords", title: "The honest ceiling", type: "prose",
        html: `The keyword map has a column most don't: what can actually be won. The brand name and “Gaggal Airport taxi” are very winnable. A bare “Dharamshala” belongs to Wikipedia, HP Tourism and the travel portals, so the pages aim at <em>Dharamshala sightseeing</em>, <em>taxi</em> and <em>how far from Gaggal</em> instead. With no prices published, the site doesn't chase “Kangra tour packages” price searches. It goes after the itinerary and “with a local driver” searches the portals don't answer.` },
      { tag: "Speed", title: "The second origin that cost the LCP", type: "prose",
        html: `Serving the hero photo from an image CDN, even at 34 KB, moved Lighthouse's mobile LCP from 1.9 to 2.7 seconds, because a second origin costs a connection before the first pixel. The hero now ships as self-hosted AVIF, with a 32 KB phone crop. A second body font had cost about 1.5 seconds of mobile LCP, so the site uses one 27 KB file. The serif in the logo is outlined into the SVG, so no serif font loads at all.` },
      { tag: "Rules", type: "list", items: ["Every page has at least three inbound links and sits within three clicks of home", "19 colour pairs must pass WCAG AA, including white text over photo scrims", "Titles of 60 characters or fewer, all unique, with one H1 and no skipped headings", "Every FAQ question in the schema must be visible on the page", "Every call and WhatsApp link must be Sahil's number", "Corners are square, enforced by the build"] },
      { tag: "The lesson", type: "prose",
        html: `For a one-person business, the website has to be as trustworthy as the person. That means saying less: no fare until he names it, no “AC” until he confirms it, and his number plate in every photograph. For a broader look at taxi sites, see <a href="/blog/taxi-tour-operator-website-pages/">what pages a taxi or tour operator website needs</a>.` },
    ],
